one with the  restaurant industry in mind  but they're certainly open to more  feedback abigail beeman global news  ottawa some relief could be coming for  restaurants in ontario where in-person  dining is banned the province wants to  slash fees that food delivery apps like  ubereats and doordash  charge in ontario those commissions can  reach up to 30 percent  the proposed law would cap delivery fees  at 15 percent and total charges it would  max out at  20 percent the aim is also to ensure  companies don't restrict their delivery  zones or cut out certain restaurants  something similar was done in new york  city  a change to the sentence of the quebec  mosque gunman coming up why a court has  ruled the original sentence  was cruel and unusual  the man who killed six people in an  attack on a mosque in quebec city has  had his sentence reduced  alexander bissonnette was originally  sentenced to life with no chance of  parole for 40 years  today quebec's highest court cut that  back to 25 years without chance of  parole  mike armstrong explains the decision and  why it likely  will be appealed it was a sentence that  from the minute it was handed down  didn't make either side happy in fact it  was appealed by both  alexander bissonette's defense team and  the crown  now bissonnette pleaded guilty to six  murders and six counts of attempted  murder traditionally that would mean six  life sentences  with no chance of parole for 25 years  but the sentencing rules  changed with legislation in 2011 and the  judge could have handed down consecutive  sentences  that's what the crown wanted no chance  of parole for 150 years  the defense wanted it concurrent capped  at just 25 years  quebec superior court justice francois  went in a completely different direction  he handed down the 25 and tacked on  another 15.  he decided that the legislation was  unconstitutional but he kind of rewrote  it in a way that he felt it would be  constitutional  well in a decision handed down thursday  the quebec court of appeal  says that was a mistake the judge didn't  have the power to add a new option  but it also went further the appeals  court is declaring the 2011 changes to  the criminal code that made consecutive  sentences possible  invalid i'm glad that the court  concluded that the legislation  could give rise to sentences that are  cruel and unusual  and it's now unconstitutional  a spokesperson for the quebec city  mosque where the attack took place  says the community is hurt by the  reduced sentence  he says they'll respect the justice  system's decision but  questions whether actual justice has  been done  while the debate is far from over  observers say it's a certainty the  question of consecutive sentences  will head to the supreme court but it is  in a way for someone like bissenet  basically academic he can ask for parole  after serving 25 years  but considering the heinous nature of  the crime  he's very unlikely to get it mike  armstrong global news  montreal vancouver's city council has  voted unanimously to ask the federal  government to  decriminalize possession of small  amounts of illicit drugs the city will  ask the federal government for an  exemption from the controlled drugs and  substances act it's part of a plan  to develop a health-focused approach to  substance use and end the stigma against  drug users  2020 is on track to be the worst year  for overdoses  last month the bc coroner reported 162  overdose deaths about five per day  if approved vancouver would be the first  jurisdiction in canada to decriminalize  small amounts of street drugs  for personal possession still ahead the  final offensive fears grow for civilians  caught up  in an ethiopian conflict  the situation in ethiopia is getting  worse tonight the country's prime  minister has ordered the army to move in  on the capital  of the tigray region in a statement abi  ahmed says the military offensive has  reached its final stage  he urges citizens to stay home and away  from potential targets  more than 40 000 ethiopians have fled to  nearby sudan  since the fighting began earlier this  month  [Music]  and this is the kind of honor usually  given to heads of state tens of  thousands of argentinians lined up for  hours to pay their respects to diego  maradona the soccer star dazzled the  world and led argentina to win the world  cup in 1986.  he died of a heart attack yesterday at  the age of 60.  we're back in a moment  germany's chancellor is calling for  european ski resorts to remain closed  until at least  early january italy and france have  shown support for the idea but austria  has voiced concern  and mountains in switzerland which is  not part of the eu are already open  ski resorts in europe were among the  first covid19  hotspots here in canada whistler black  home in bc  opened for business today it's got the  snow and some pandemic protocols in  place but  what's missing are the tourists and  their money global's paul johnson is in  whistler paul donna you know this was  never certain and of course things could  change very quickly  but north america's biggest ski resort  and canada's most well-known  open for business this morning and their  intention is  to try to stay open for the entire  season  what's making this possible is the way  they've organized sales  and how they're managing numbers on the  mountains  their formula is this they know what  their capacity is for getting people up  and down on the lifts and gondolas  while observing social distancing that  tells them  how many people they can accommodate and  those spots  then have to be reserved online and in  advance  so there are no walk-up ticket sales  right now the executives here say it's a  work in progress  but they're asking for people to be  patient the team has done an amazing job  and just countless hours planning and  prepping and reimagining  everything that we do so that we could  operate and  our goal for the year is to get open and  to stay open for the whole year and uh  today is the first step  first day back so stoked we're open  you know it's obviously a bit different  than we all hope  but the snow is better than last year  of course the other big question here is  what's going to be the story  for all of the restaurants bars hotels  and service businesses  that rely on big visitor numbers in  order to survive  well that's most certainly going to  depend on the status  of the various travel restrictions  across canada  and internationally and whether at least  some of those  might be lifted at some point this  season but bottom line  the biggest economic engines of this  place are the two mountains themselves  and with the lifts running at least  there's some hope  donna all right paul johnson and wessler  thanks  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm donna friesen  we leave you tonight with an update on  rocky he's the little owl rescued from  the christmas tree at rockefeller center  in new york city while  rocky has had his fill of the big apple  and after 10 days recovering at a  wildlife center  there he goes he's back in the forest  where he belongs  thanks for watching take care of  yourselves bye-bye
 

